279e0349 [gemini-3.8-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪣 0.7° $0.047 175.8KB 6.71s
279e0349 [gemini-3.8-flash] #A built-in console editor inside the relay environment is an interesting addition, especially since
279e0349 [gemini-3.8-flash] #`slopprompt.ts` already implements raw terminal I/O.
279e0349 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
279e0349 [gemini-3.8-flash] #When considering a built-in editor versus invoking an external editor (`$EDITOR`), there are a
279e0349 [gemini-3.8-flash] #couple of architectural directions to evaluate:
279e0349 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
279e0349 [gemini-3.8-flash] #1. Minimal Line / Chunk Editor (ed / ex style)
279e0349 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• How it works: Works within standard scrolling terminal output without needing full-screen
279e0349 [gemini-3.8-flash] #alternate buffers. Commands allow viewing a window of lines, replacing lines by index, inserting
279e0349 [gemini-3.8-flash] #text, appending, or regex replacement.
279e0349 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Advantages:
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #  * Simple state machine.
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #  * Preserves conversation/terminal scrollback cleanly.
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #  * Fits neatly into existing line-based input routines without complex terminal redraw loops.
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #  * Very easy to script or automate via LLM tool calls.
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #2. Full-Screen Visual Editor (nano / kilo style)
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #• How it works: Switches terminal to the alternate screen buffer (`\x1b[?1049h`), captures
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #raw keystrokes (navigation arrows, backspace, page up/down), renders a status bar, and redraws rows
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #on change. On exit (`Ctrl+Q` or `Ctrl+X`), switches back to the primary screen (`\x1b[?1049l`).
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Core Components:
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #  * Buffer State: An array of lines (`string[]`) or a gap buffer, cursor position
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #(`cursorRow`, `cursorCol`), scroll offsets (`rowOffset`, `colOffset`), and a dirty flag.
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #  * Input Dispatcher: A named key-handling loop decoding escape sequences (cursor movement,
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #backspace, Enter, delete, page up/down, key combos).
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #  * Screen Renderer: Clears screen, positions cursor via ANSI sequences (`\x1b[H`,
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #`\x1b[<r>;<c>H`), renders text rows clipped to `Deno.consoleSize()`, and paints a footer/status bar
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #showing filename, modified state, and shortcuts.
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #  * Lifecycle & Cleanup: Rigorous signal handling (`SIGINT`, `SIGTERM`) and `try...finally`
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #blocks to guarantee the terminal exits raw mode and restores the primary screen buffer if anything
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #throws.
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #3. Subprocess Delegation (`$EDITOR` fallback)
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Temporarily pause raw mode, spawn the user's preferred editor (`$EDITOR`, `nano`, `vim`, or
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #`micro`) with `stdin`, `stdout`, and `stderr` set to `"inherit"`, await process completion, and
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #then resume `relay`'s prompt loop.
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #Would you prefer a lightweight inline/line-oriented editor, a full-screen raw-mode visual editor,
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #or spawning an external editor process?
279e034a [gemini-3.8-flash] #
