2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪣 0.7° $0.001 27.87KB 2.54s
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #Partly — but be honest with yourself about which part.
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #The line does two jobs:
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #- States one rule — no inline call chains. That transfers exactly and only that.
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #- Signals a register — terse, unhedged, no pleasantries. A reader who sees it will infer
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #"this codebase values brevity," and may mimic the style. That's real, but it's a vibe, not a spec.
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #What it can't do is inform rules it doesn't mention. A reader won't derive "tabs, not
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #spaces," "single-statement guards may sit on the `if` line," or "prefer named functions over
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #lambdas" from one line about call chains — they'll just guess, and guess inconsistently.
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #So: tight is good for the rules you actually name. If you want other things covered, they need to
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #be named too (or pointed at in a `STYLE.md`). The comment's brevity doesn't widen its scope — it
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #just sets the tone for whatever you do spell out.
2848b93a [deepseek-flash] #
