28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #`umount`
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #• What it does: Flushes any pending data from RAM to the physical disk and safely
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #disconnects the filesystem from the directory tree.
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Analogy: Using "Safely Remove Hardware" or closing the door before disconnecting.
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Usage:
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #  sudo umount /mnt/storage
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #Why both were used in the previous step:
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #To verify that your `/etc/fstab` entry is written correctly without rebooting your machine:
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #1. `sudo umount /mnt/storage` temporarily unhooks the drive.
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #2. `sudo mount -a` tells the system to read `/etc/fstab` and mount everything listed there.
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #If the drive reappears under `/mnt/storage`, it confirms that the drive will mount automatically on
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #every reboot without syntax errors.
28b36b7d [gemini-3.8-flash] #
