28dbe1f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #Note on Piping
28dbe1f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #If you're piping `ls` output to another command, you often don't even need `-1` — `ls`
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #automatically switches to one-per-line when it detects the output isn't a terminal:
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #ls | grep ".js"
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #Truly Bare (No Color, No Indicators)
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #If your shell has `ls` aliased with extra flags (common with `alias ls='ls --color=auto'` or
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #similar), you might still see color codes or symbols like `*`, `/`, `@`. For a completely plain,
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #unaliased result:
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #\ls -1
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #The leading backslash bypasses any alias and calls the real `ls` binary directly.
28dbe1f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
