28dc7453 [claude-sonnet-5] #Fair enough — no need to excuse anything. You're right that "unused software = larger attack
28dc7453 [claude-sonnet-5] #surface" is a legitimate security principle, not paranoia. Every installed package is something
28dc7453 [claude-sonnet-5] #that could theoretically have a vulnerability, and "just leave it, it's harmless" isn't great
28dc7453 [claude-sonnet-5] #security advice even if the immediate risk is low. Let's get it actually gone.
28dc7453 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7453 [claude-sonnet-5] #Removing Falkon Properly
28dc7453 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7453 [claude-sonnet-5] #Step 1: Remove Falkon and the Metapackage Together
28dc7453 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7453 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #sudo pacman -Rns falkon kde-network-meta
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #This clears the dependency conflict since you're removing both at once. The `-n` also nukes
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #leftover config files, and `-s` cleans up orphaned dependencies that only existed to support
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #Falkon.
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #Step 2: Verify It's Actually Gone
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #pacman -Qs falkon
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #No output = fully removed.
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #Step 3: Clean Up Any Leftover User Config
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #rm -rf ~/.config/falkon ~/.cache/falkon
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #Step 4: Check for Orphaned Dependencies
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #Sometimes removing one package leaves "orphans" — packages that were only pulled in as
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #dependencies:
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #pacman -Qtdq
28dc7454 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #If it lists anything, review it and remove with:
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #sudo pacman -Rns $(pacman -Qtdq)
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #About `kde-network-meta`
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #Worth knowing: removing that metapackage won't break your existing network tools (already-installed
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #apps stay installed). It just means future `pacman -Syu` won't automatically pull in whatever new
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #apps get added to that group later. If you want tighter control over what's installed anyway,
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #that's arguably a net positive for your threat model, not a downside.
28dc7455 [claude-sonnet-5] #
