28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #Thanks for sharing both files. Here's what stands out:
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #`stereo.log` — Mostly Routine, One Notable Bit
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #- Standard headless process startup/reconnect cycle to Valkey (Redis-compatible), with
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #`CONNECTIONCOUNT` climbing steadily from 207 → 365.
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #- The interesting part is this exchange:
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #VALKEY:GET wiki.banner=>"simon was here"
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #VALKEY:SET wiki.banner "simon was here\nhe hit the edit button\nand he typed some stuff\nhe did"
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #...
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #VALKEY:SET wiki.banner "simon was here\n>\n* he hit the edit button\n* and he typed some stuff\n* he did\n"
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #Someone (or an automated test) is live-editing a `wiki.banner` key, and the second edit adds
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #Markdown-style formatting (`>` blockquote, `*` bullet points). If this banner renders somewhere in
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #your UI, worth confirming that's expected content and not an unauthorized edit — the phrasing ("hit
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #the edit button... typed some stuff") reads like a manual test/demo rather than malicious activity,
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #but flagging it since it's a write operation into shared state.
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #`valkeys1.txt` — This One Needs a Flag ⚠️
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #This file contains live-looking session tokens in plaintext:
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #{"connection":"headless.h[641]","ticks":t...,"token":"xvOjDbmWx8Pl...=="}
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #A few things worth raising:
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #1. These look like auth/session tokens, not just IDs. If they're used for anything beyond
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #internal diagnostics (auth headers, API keys, session resumption), having 200+ of them sitting in a
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #log file — and now shared outside your system — is a real exposure risk. If any of these are still
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #valid/active, I'd treat this as something to rotate.
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #2. Tick value inconsistency. The `stereo.h` entries have tick values in a sane, consistent
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #range (billions — looks like nanoseconds since some recent epoch/boot). The `headless.h` entries
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #jump wildly — from small values up to `t68702244871936` (trillions). That's a ~10,000x range
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #difference for what should presumably be the same clock source. Worth checking whether:
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #   - Different connections are using different tick bases (e.g., monotonic clock vs wall-clock
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #nanoseconds)
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #   - There's a counter overflow/reset bug somewhere in whatever's generating these
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #   - Some of these are actually stale/malformed entries mixed into the same file
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #3. Session timestamps are in 2026. The `sessions` section's `m` field (Unix ms) resolves to
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #dates in 2026, e.g. `m1768553931000` ≈ Jan 2026. If that's not intentional (e.g., a test
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #environment with the system clock set forward), it's worth checking `date` on that machine — this
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #could otherwise cause downstream issues with token expiry, cache TTLs, or log ordering logic.
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #• Is any of this new to you, or does the token/tick inconsistency match something you were
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #already suspecting?* Also — are those tokens currently live/production, or is this a dev/test
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #instance? That'll determine whether the exposure concern is urgent or just a "clean up your
28dd4a4f [claude-sonnet-5] #logging" note for later.
