28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #Fair enough — hash-based is solid until the one thing that actually breaks it: stale HTML
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #referencing an old hash after you've removed that file.
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #The usual pattern to avoid that:
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #- Keep old hashed files around for a while (don't delete immediately) or
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #- Make sure your HTML/deploy step always updates in lockstep with the new hash, so there's no
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #window where a page references a hash that's already gone
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #As long as the HTML and the hashed asset are deployed together, it holds up fine indefinitely. It
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #only "breaks" if something references a version that's already been purged/deleted — which is a
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #deploy discipline issue, not a CDN one.
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #Good plan either way — full purge now, hash paths later when you're ready.
28de6105 [claude-sonnet-5] #
