2905e2a6 [gpt-5.6-sol] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 gpt-5.6-sol 🌀 🪠 🧊 $0.023 14.15KB 8.06s
2905e2a6 [gpt-5.6-sol] #Yes—Deno can safely call Bunny.net APIs using an API key, provided the key stays
2905e2a6 [gpt-5.6-sol] #server-side.
2905e2a6 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
2905e2a6 [gpt-5.6-sol] #- Store it in a Deno environment variable or your hosting platform’s secret manager.
2905e2a6 [gpt-5.6-sol] #- Do not embed it in browser JavaScript, HTML, public repositories, or responses.
2905e2a6 [gpt-5.6-sol] #- Use the appropriate Bunny credential: account API key, Storage Zone password, or
2905e2a6 [gpt-5.6-sol] #token-authentication key.
2905e2a6 [gpt-5.6-sol] #- Prefer a narrowly scoped credential where Bunny supports one. The main account API key may have
2905e2a6 [gpt-5.6-sol] #broad access.
2905e2a6 [gpt-5.6-sol] #- Send requests from your Deno backend using `fetch`.
2905e2a6 [gpt-5.6-sol] #- If using Deno Deploy, configure the key as a project secret.
2905e2a7 [gpt-5.6-sol] #- For client downloads, generate short-lived signed URLs/tokens server-side rather than exposing
2905e2a7 [gpt-5.6-sol] #the key.
2905e2a7 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
2905e2a7 [gpt-5.6-sol] #If a key has already appeared in client code or a repository, rotate it in Bunny’s dashboard.
2905e2a7 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
