2950fe4e [skid@archlinux] if i want default height map to include lidar height is there already a setting
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.010 430.9KB 17.59s
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #Short answer: no, there's no runtime setting that switches on LiDAR. The topo grid choice is
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #data-driven from `json/tiles.json`, and the code will fetch whatever `toponame` each layer entry
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #names.
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #Where the choice actually lives
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #`TopoTile.fetchTopoTile` in `wire_topo.js` builds the URL from the layer's `toponame`:
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #if("toponame" in layer){
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #	const src="topogrid/topo-"+layer.toponame+"/"+level+"/"+x+"/"+y+".raw";
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #	...
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #	this.fetchTileRaw(src2);
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #	notopo=false;
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #}
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #So whether you get LiDAR-derived tiles, contour-derived DEM, or something else is decided entirely
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #by what `tiles.json` says. There's no mip variant for topo either — unlike aerial, which picks
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #`TopoMipName[res]` / `AppPlatform.MipName` (`""` vs `"sd-"`). The `.raw` path is fixed.
2950ff6e [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #The only two flags in `AppRuntime` (`wire_grid.js`) are:
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #let AppRuntime={
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #	UseAerial:true,
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #	UseLINZ:true
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #};
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #`UseAerial` gates the JPEG overlay, `UseLINZ` gates the `roagrid` drawing JSON (roads, roofs,
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #coast, etc.). Neither touches the topo `.raw`.
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #The combining mechanism already exists
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #`TopoSource.listSources` returns every layer in `this.visible` that matches `level` and bounds. If
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #`tiles.json` lists two topo layers for the same tile, both `.raw` files are fetched and handed to
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #the worker, which merges them via `orBytes`:
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #function createTopo(){
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #	const bytes=heightmaps[0]||[];
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #	const heightmap=AllocBumpmap(bytes);
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #	heightmap.setBytes(bytes);
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #	const n=heightmaps.length;
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #	for(let i=1;i<n;i++){
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #		if(heightmaps[i].length==bytes.length){
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #			heightmap.orBytes(heightmaps[i]);
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #		}
2950ff6f [deepseek-flash] #		...
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #	}
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #}
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #Two important caveats about that path:
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #- `orBytes` is overwrite where nonzero, not max or blend. It only writes where the incoming
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #source has `h != 0`. So order in `sourceList` decides who wins — and that order is just the order
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #of `tiles.aerial.concat(tiles.topo, tiles.roa)` in the `TopoSource` constructor. If you add a LiDAR
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #layer alongside a base DEM, whichever is later in that concatenation overwrites the other on
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #overlapping pixels.
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #- It requires the two heightmaps to have the same byte length (`heightmaps[i].length ==
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #bytes.length`). Different resolutions won't merge — the else branch is commented out.
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #So "include LiDAR" via the existing path means: put a LiDAR-derived topo entry in `tiles.json` at
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #the right `layer` and with the right `bounds`, either replacing the existing topo entry or adding
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #it after it.
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #If you want an explicit switch
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #Adding one is straightforward and mirrors `UseAerial`. In `wire_grid.js`:
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #let AppRuntime={
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #	UseAerial:true,
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #	UseLINZ:true,
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #	UseLiDAR:false
2950ff70 [deepseek-flash] #};
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #Then in `fetchTopoTile`, gate the LiDAR-named layer:
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #if("toponame" in layer){
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #	const useLidar = layer.toponame.startsWith("lidar");
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #	if(!useLidar || AppRuntime.UseLiDAR){
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #		const src="topogrid/topo-"+layer.toponame+"/"+level+"/"+x+"/"+y+".raw";
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #		...
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #		this.fetchTileRaw(src2);
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #	}
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #}
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #That assumes your `tiles.json` naming convention identifies LiDAR entries — check what the
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #`toponame` values actually are before committing to a predicate. If they're opaque (`"ak17"`,
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #`"nz1"`), you'll need a per-entry boolean like `layer.lidar: true` instead.
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #One thing that will bite you
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #`heal()` is commented out in `worker_baker.js`:
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #// simon come here and fix up flora
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #//	heightmap.heal(n);
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff71 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #LiDAR coverage is patchy — swaths, water masks, no-data areas all come back as `h == 0`. The
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #worker's `setBytes` converts those to `topo[i] = -TopoLevel`, which is a full 16384 units below the
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #topo origin. On screen that's a deep pit, not a flat gap. If you enable LiDAR and see scattered
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #depressions, that's this. `heal()` was meant to fill them (the body is a stub — it counts zeros but
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #doesn't actually repair), so you'd be implementing it, not just uncommenting it.
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #The main-thread `Bumpmap.setBytes` in `wire_topo.js` doesn't have the `else` branch, so the
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #no-baker path leaves zeros as `h = 0` (interpreting as sea-level topo offset). Different symptom,
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #same root cause.
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #What to check first
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #Open `json/tiles.json` and look at the `topo` array entries — specifically the `toponame` fields
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #and the `layer` numbers. That will tell you whether LiDAR is already in the default set, whether
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #it's a separate entry you can toggle, or whether you need to add one. Without that file I can't
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #tell you which.
2950ff72 [deepseek-flash] #
297aec63 [skid@archlinux] why is flora layer so dark?
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.015 557.2KB 27.33s
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #Two separate problems combine here, and the primary one is not in the palette.
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #Primary cause: flora has zero normals
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #The flora surface is posted by `worker_baker.js` with normals explicitly zeroed:
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #v.push([mx,my,mz,c, 0,0,0,0]);
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #//                 ^^^^^^^^^ nx,ny,nz all zero
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #Compare with `CreateTopoSurface` in the same worker — also zero normals, but it gets fixed up
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #afterwards in `TopoTile.onBump`:
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #const surface=CreateTopoSurface(128,16,GroundPen,bump);
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #const zfactor=4.6/(this.level*8.0);
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #surface.calcNormals(zfactor);   // <-- fixes the tile surface
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #this.topoSurface=surface;
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #Flora has no equivalent. `onFlora` just stores it:
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #onFlora(verts,indices){
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #    this.floraSurface=new Surface(verts,indices,[]);
297aee33 [deepseek-flash] #}
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #And `addTileShape` does not compute normals the way `addFlatShape` does:
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #addFlatShape(surface,origin,count){
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #    shape.addFlatSurface(surface);   // -> flatifyVerts -> calcNormals
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #    ...
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #}
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #addTileShape(surface,basemap,scratch,origin){
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #    ...
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #    shape.addSurface(surface);       // raw verts32, no normal pass
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #    ...
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #}
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #The tile shader has `sun` as a uniform and `normal` as an attribute, so lighting is `dot(normal,
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #sun)`. With normals all zero, that's zero — you get ambient only, which reads as a very dark flat
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #surface. That's the symptom.
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #Fix: compute normals for the flora surface, using the same `zfactor` expression as `onBump` so
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #shading is consistent with the terrain underneath.
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #onFlora(verts,indices){
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #    this.floraSurface=new Surface(verts,indices,[]);
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #    const zfactor=4.6/(this.level*8.0);
297aee34 [deepseek-flash] #    this.floraSurface.calcNormals(zfactor);
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #}
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #If you'd rather keep the main thread free, call `calcNormals` in the worker's `postFlora` before
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #posting — but `calcNormals` uses the `Surface` class, and `worker_baker.js` doesn't import
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #`wire_surface.js`, so you'd have to duplicate the routine. Doing it in `onFlora` is the smaller
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #change.
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #Secondary: `FloraPen=32` is out of palette range
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #`RoaPalette` has 30 entries (indices 0–29). `FloraPen` is defined as `32` in `worker_baker.js`:
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #const FloraPen=32;
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #WebGL uniform-array out-of-bounds reads are undefined; in practice most drivers return 0, so the
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #flora colour term collapses to black on top of the normal problem. If your intended colour was a
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #green, use one of the palette indices that actually is green — 4 (`G,B,G,A`), 15 (`G,B,G,A` with
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #alpha), or 24 (`0,G,0,A`). Given the terrain uses `GroundPen=1` and the tree-like flora sits on
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #grass, `15` is a reasonable pick.
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #If you change `FloraPen`, do it in one place and pass it through to `postFlora`; right now it's a
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #hard-coded constant on the worker side.
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #Why it looks "dark" rather than "missing"
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #The flora geometry is still emitted — that's why you see a shape — but with NdotL at zero and a
297aee35 [deepseek-flash] #black palette entry, it's only the alpha-blended fringe against the terrain below that keeps it
297aee36 [deepseek-flash] #visible at all. Once you add the `calcNormals` call, you should see it light up immediately; fixing
297aee36 [deepseek-flash] #the palette index after that will give it the colour you actually intended.
297aee36 [deepseek-flash] #
297aee36 [roha] [RELAY] commitShares starCount 0
297b503c [skid@archlinux] have set pen to 31, how do i change from what is now transparent green
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.011 560.7KB 13.54s
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #31 is still out of range — `RoaPalette` in `wire_core.js` has 30 entries, indices 0–29. Whatever
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #colour you're seeing is coming from the driver's undefined-behaviour read of the `palette` uniform,
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #which is why it's not what you'd expect.
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #How the colour actually gets picked
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #The vertex `xyzc` attribute carries a colour byte in `c`. In `worker_baker.js`, `postFlora` writes
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #that byte from the `c` argument, which is `FloraPen`. The tile fragment shader uses it to index a
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #`palette` uniform:
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #this.RawPalette=new Float32Array(RoaPalette)   // wire_core.js
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #layer.setFloats("palette",gpu.RawPalette);     // wire_graphics.js
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #Each palette entry is 4 floats (r,g,b,a). So both the colour and the transparency come from
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #the same index — the 4th float is alpha.
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #The green entries, with their alpha
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #Reading `RoaPalette`, the entries that read as green are:
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #
297b511b [deepseek-flash] #╭─────────┬──────────────────────┬─────────┬────────────────────────────────╮
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #│  Index  │  RGB                 │  Alpha  │  Look                          │
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #├─────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #│  4      │  (0.40, 0.70, 0.40)  │  0.94   │  muted green, **opaque**       │
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #│  9      │  (0.40, 0.94, 0.70)  │  0.94   │  mint, **opaque**              │
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #│  15     │  (0.40, 0.70, 0.40)  │  0.52   │  muted green, **transparent**  │
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #│  20     │  (0.40, 0.94, 0.70)  │  0.52   │  mint, **transparent**         │
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #│  24     │  (0, 0.40, 0)        │  0.52   │  dark green, **transparent**   │
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #│  25     │  (0, 0.40, 0.70)     │  0.52   │  teal, **transparent**         │
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #╰─────────┴──────────────────────┴─────────┴────────────────────────────────╯
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #The `O` constant is `0.94` (opaque-ish), the `A` constant is `0.52` (transparent). So the
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #transparent green you're seeing is one of the `A`-alpha entries — most likely 15 or 24.
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #If you want an opaque green, set `FloraPen` to `4` or `9`.
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #If none of those is the green you want
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #Add a new entry to `RoaPalette` in `wire_core.js`:
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #const RoaPalette=[
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #	...
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #	G,G,G,A,      // 29
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #	0.15,0.55,0.15,0.94   // 30 - new opaque forest green
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #];
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #
297b511c [deepseek-flash] #Then set `FloraPen=30` in `worker_baker.js`.
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #Two caveats:
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #- The tile shader declares `uniform vec4 palette[N]`. If `N` is currently 30, the 31st entry will
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #be dropped or the program will fail to link depending on the driver. Check the shader source and
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #bump `N` if you're adding beyond index 29.
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #- `RawPalette` is `new Float32Array(RoaPalette)`, so it picks up the new length automatically — no
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #change needed there.
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #Also still worth doing
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #The transparent green will still look wrong under lighting until you add the `calcNormals` call in
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #`TopoTile.onFlora` — the flora verts ship with `0,0,0` normals, so NdotL is zero and the shader
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #falls back to ambient. Fix that first, then the palette change will read as intended rather than as
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #a flat dark silhouette.
297b511d [deepseek-flash] #
297b597f [skid@archlinux] is there code for removing building shape from flora layer, 
297b5aca [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.013 563.8KB 20.21s
297b5aca [deepseek-flash] #No — nothing in the codebase removes building footprints from the flora surface. The pieces needed
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #are all present, but they're not wired together.
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #Why buildings currently show up in flora
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #Flora is generated in the worker, from the merged heightmap, before any drawing data reaches the
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #tile:
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #// worker_baker.js createTopo()
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #heightmap.postFlora(256,8,FloraPen,22);   // <-- flora built here
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #heightmap.post();
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #The mask inside `postFlora` is purely `diff`-based:
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #const m=this.pickHeightMinMax(x*p,y*p);
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #if(m.min>floraMin){        // floraMin = 22
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #	const h1=m.max;
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #	a[y][x]=h0+h1;
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #}
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #`pickHeight` reads `this.diff`, and on LiDAR that second channel is high around building edges, so
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #those cells pass the `>22` test. There is no reference to roofs, ground, or any exclusion mask
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #anywhere in the worker.
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #What already exists that does the right thing
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acb [deepseek-flash] #`TopoTile.digOutline` (in `wire_topo.js`) is invoked per roof outline when `build_roof2` is on. It
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #calls `Bits.digTopo`, which does exactly the zeroing you'd want:
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #// wire_util.js
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #heightmap.setGround(tx+bx, ty+by, depth);
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #heightmap.setHeight(tx+bx, ty+by, 0);   // <-- diff becomes 0
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #`setHeight` writes to `diff`, so any cell inside the footprint (plus a `border` halo) fails the
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #`m.min > floraMin` test. If `TopoFloraSurface` ran after `digTopo`, buildings would already
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #be excluded — no new exclusion logic needed.
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #And `TopoFloraSurface` does exist, in `wire_core.js`, marked "moved to baker" but never deleted:
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #// moved to baker
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #function TopoFloraSurface(count,mag,c,topo,floraMin){
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #	...
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #}
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #It is byte-for-byte the same algorithm as the worker's `postFlora`, minus the
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #`postMessage`/transfer at the end.
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #The ordering problem
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #The worker runs at fetch time. `digOutline` runs at mesh time, much later, and only inside
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #`Builder.buildTile`:
297b5acc [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #meshTile(tile,home){
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #	...
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #	const surfaces=this.builder.buildTile(tile,2);   // digOutline fires here
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #	...
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #	const floraSurface=tile.floraSurface;            // <-- already built by worker
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #	const floramesh=layer0.addTileShape(floraSurface,...);
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #}
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #So even with `build_roof2` enabled, the flora surface was constructed before the dig and is
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #unaffected. Regenerating it after `buildTile` is the whole fix.
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #Minimal patch
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #In `wire_graphics.js` `meshTile`, after `buildTile` returns and before the flora mesh is uploaded,
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #rebuild the flora surface on the main thread:
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #const surfaces=this.builder.buildTile(tile,2);
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #tile.allocWallmap();
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #tile.staticmeesh=layer0.addStaticShapes(surfaces,tile.scratch,tile.wallmap,origin);
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #const topo=tile.topoSurface;
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #tile.tilemesh=layer0.addTileShape(topo,tile.basemap,tile.scratch,origin);
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acd [deepseek-flash] #// flora is rebuilt here so that digOutline has already zeroed diff
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #// over building footprints
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #const floraSurface=TopoFloraSurface(256,8,FloraPen,tile.heightmap,22);
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #const zfactor=4.6/(tile.level*8.0);
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #floraSurface.calcNormals(zfactor);
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #tile.floraSurface=floraSurface;
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #const floramesh=layer0.addTileShape(floraSurface,tile.basemap,tile.scratch,origin);
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #tile.floramesh=floramesh;
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #`FloraPen` is defined in `wire_topo.js`, which is loaded alongside `wire_graphics.js`, so it's in
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #scope.
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #Caveats
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #- `build_roof2` must be on. It's `false` by default in `Builder.buildTile`, so `digOutline`
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #never runs. Flip it to `true` and you'll get the roof-block behaviour — including the basement
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #fill, the roof wall pixel accounting, and the `context.clip()` region, all of which will now
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #execute. If you only want the dig and not the rest, the cleaner change is to split the block: call
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #`tile.digOutline(outline,BasementDepth,40)` in a small loop before the `build_roof2` block, leaving
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #`build_roof2` off.
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #- The worker still generates its flora. With this patch, `tile.floraSurface` is overwritten
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #on the main thread, so the worker's version is discarded — but it was still computed and
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #transferred, which is wasted work. To reclaim it, delete the `heightmap.postFlora(...)` call in
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #`worker_baker.js createTopo()` and drop the `flora` case from `TileInstance.onMessage`. Otherwise
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #you're paying for two flora passes per tile.
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #- `border=40` is generous. `digOutline` takes a `border` in heightmap pixels, so each
297b5ace [deepseek-flash] #footprint gets a 40-pixel halo of zeroed `diff`. At LiDAR resolution that's several metres —
297b5acf [deepseek-flash] #probably fine for tree exclusion, but tune it once you can see the result.
297b5acf [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acf [deepseek-flash] #- Normals. The worker's `postFlora` ships `0,0,0` normals, and `TopoFloraSurface` inherits
297b5acf [deepseek-flash] #that (`v.push([mx,my,mz,c, 0,0,0,0])`). Calling `calcNormals` after rebuilding is what lights the
297b5acf [deepseek-flash] #surface — otherwise the flora will still look dark, independent of any exclusion.
297b5acf [deepseek-flash] #
297b5acf [deepseek-flash] #- `AppRuntime.UseLINZ` and `level<3` gate whether roof drawings are fetched at all in
297b5acf [deepseek-flash] #`fetchTopoTile`. Both are already `true`/`<3` in the default config, but worth confirming for
297b5acf [deepseek-flash] #whichever level you're viewing.
297b5acf [deepseek-flash] #
297b60a7 [skid@archlinux] cant i post once footprints are dug?
297b61df [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.011 569.0KB 17.21s
297b61df [deepseek-flash] #Yes — and it's the cleaner shape. But it means moving the dig into the worker, because the worker
297b61df [deepseek-flash] #can't do it after the fact.
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #Why the worker can't do it post-hoc
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #`Bumpmap.post()` transfers the buffers away:
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #const transfer=[topo.buffer,diff.buffer,ground.buffer];
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #postMessage(message,transfer);
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #After that the worker's `topo`/`diff`/`ground` are detached. Even if you sent a `dig` command
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #later, there's nothing to dig into. So "post once footprints are dug" only works if the dig happens
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #in the worker, before the transfer.
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #What that requires
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #Three things move to the worker side:
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #- `Bits` from `wire_util.js` — the polygon rasteriser. Currently only loaded on the main
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #thread.
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #- `TopoTile.digOutline` — or a shared version of it. It's ~30 lines and reads only from the
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #heightmap and the outline.
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #- A protocol change — the worker must not `post()` until it has received the roof outlines.
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #And one thing has to be reordered on the main thread: `onDrawingResponse` must forward the roof
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #outlines to the worker, and `createTopo()` in the worker must defer its `postFlora` + `post` until
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #those arrive.
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #New flow
297b61e0 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #main                          worker
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #────                          ──────
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #fetchTileRaw  ──heightmap──▶  accumulate
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #fetchDrawing                  (hold, don't post yet)
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #  onDrawingResponse
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #    extract ROOF outlines
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #                ──dig───────▶  apply dig to topo/diff/ground
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #                               postFlora(256,8,FloraPen,22)
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #                               post({bump, flora})  ──▶ main
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #                              (transfer buffers now)
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #Because the dig runs first, both the bump surface and the flora surface are built from the same dug
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #state. The main thread's `onBump` creates `topoSurface` from an already-dug heightmap — no rebuild
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #in `meshTile` needed. The whole "rebuild after buildTile" workaround disappears.
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #The two messages become one
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #Right now `postFlora` and `post` each fire their own `postMessage`. Collapse them:
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #function postAll(){
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #	const message=[{
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #		command:"bump",
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #		topo:this.topo, diff:this.diff, ground:this.ground,
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #		floraVerts:floraVerts, floraIndices:floraIndices
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #	}];
297b61e1 [deepseek-flash] #	const transfer=[
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #		this.topo.buffer, this.diff.buffer, this.ground.buffer,
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #		floraVerts.buffer, floraIndices.buffer
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #	];
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #	postMessage(message,transfer);
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #}
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #Then `TileInstance.onMessage` handles one case instead of two, and `hasLoaded()` loses the
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #`topoCount == heightmaps.length` / `floraSurface` conditions — the tile is loaded the moment the
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #single message arrives.
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #The gate on the main thread
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #The worker only knows when to build once both the topo and the drawing have landed.
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #`TopoTile.hasLoaded()` already tracks both (`topoCount == heightmaps.length` and `drawingCount ==
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #drawings.length`), but the worker's `refresh()` currently fires on the topo count alone. So the
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #worker needs to know to wait.
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #Two ways:
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #- Send a `dig` command from the main thread once `hasLoaded()` is true, carrying the
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #extracted roof outlines. Worker applies the dig, then builds. Simple, one-shot, no extra state.
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #- Send `topoCount` and `drawingCount` to the worker and let it decide. More coupling, not
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #worth it.
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #The first is cleaner. `TopoGrid.refreshTiles` / `populateHome` already gate on `hasLoaded()`, so
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #you have a natural place to fire the dig message.
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e2 [deepseek-flash] #Gotchas
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #- `BasementDepth` is 4. That's a near-invisible dig. The outline points are halved before
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #hitting `Bits` (`o[0]/2`), and `digTopo` writes `depth` directly into `ground`. At `4` the surface
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #barely moves. Bump it to something like `200` before you can tell it's working.
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #- `heightmaps.length=0` in `createTopo`. If you defer the build, don't clear until after
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #the dig. The merged `heightmap` object survives, but the raw `heightmaps` array is what the count
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #is compared against.
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #- `fetchTileRaw` bumps `topoCount` on the main thread, and that count is what the worker is
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #told via `bakeTopo(count)`. If drawings arrive first (common — they're small JSON, topo is a big
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #binary), the dig message can arrive before `topoCount` is satisfied. Either buffer the dig in the
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #worker until the topo count is met, or gate on the main thread so the dig is only sent once
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #`hasLoaded()` is true. The latter is simpler.
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #- `level<3` gate. `fetchTopoTile` only fetches `roaname` drawings when `level<3`. So at
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #level 3+ there are no roof outlines and the dig is a no-op. That's fine — the flora just comes out
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #un-dug, same as today.
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #- `Bits.poly` clamps `x` but not `y`. A negative `y` writes off the end of the typed array
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #silently, and `digTopo` then toggles the wrong region. Roof outlines near the tile edge will hit
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #this. Fix the clamp before relying on the dig.
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #- Duplicate `Bits` and `digOutline`. If you don't want to maintain two copies, load
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #`wire_util.js` into the worker via `importScripts`, and factor `digOutline` into a free function
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #that both the worker and `TopoTile` call. That's the tidier option, at the cost of a small
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #refactor.
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e3 [deepseek-flash] #Is it worth it
297b61e4 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e4 [deepseek-flash] #Compared to rebuilding flora on the main thread after `buildTile`:
297b61e4 [deepseek-flash] #
297b61e4 [deepseek-flash] #╭──────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────╮
297b61e4 [deepseek-flash] #│                                                  │  Main-thread rebuild  │  Worker dig                   │
297b61e4 [deepseek-flash] #├──────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
297b61e4 [deepseek-flash] #│  Flora passes per tile                           │  2                    │  1                            │
297b61e4 [deepseek-flash] #│  Code churn                                      │  ~6 lines             │  ~120 lines, protocol change  │
297b61e4 [deepseek-flash] #│  Ordering guarantees                             │  implicit             │  explicit                     │
297b61e4 [deepseek-flash] #│  Fits existing "everything in the worker" shape  │  no                   │  yes                          │
297b61e4 [deepseek-flash] #╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────╯
297b61e4 [deepseek-flash] #
