2986fbdc [roha]    Short answer: no, there's no runtime setting that switches on LiDAR. The topo grid choice is
2986fbdc [roha]    data-driven from `json/tiles.json`, and the code will fetch whatever `toponame` each layer
2986fbdc [roha]    entry names. ## Where the choice actually lives `TopoTile.fetchTopoTile` in `wire_topo.js`
2986fbdc [roha]    builds the URL from the layer's `toponame`: ```js if("toponame" in layer){ const
2986fbdc [roha]    src="topogrid/topo-"+layer.toponame+"/"+level+"/"+x+"/"+y+".raw"; ... this.fetchTileRaw(src2);
2986fbdc [roha]    notopo=false; } ``` So whether you get LiDAR-derived tiles, contour-derived DEM, or something
2986fbdc [roha]    else is decided entirely by what `tiles.json` says. There's no mip variant for topo either —
2986fbdc [roha]    unlike aerial, which picks `TopoMipName[res]` / `AppPlatform.MipName` (`""` vs `"sd-"`). The
2986fbdc [roha]    `.raw` path is fixed. The only two flags in `AppRuntime` (`wire_grid.js`) are: ```js let
2986fbdc [roha]    AppRuntime={ UseAerial:true, UseLINZ:true }; ``` `UseAerial` gates the JPEG overlay, `UseLINZ`
2986fbdc [roha]    gates the `roagrid` drawing JSON (roads, roofs, coast, etc.). Neither touches the topo `.raw`.
2986fbdc [roha]    ## The combining mechanism already exists `TopoSource.listSources` returns every layer in
2986fbdc [roha]    `this.visible` that matches `level` and bounds. If `tiles.json` lists two topo layers for the
2986fbdd [roha]    same tile, both `.raw` files are fetched and handed to the worker, which merges them via
2986fbdd [roha]    `orBytes`: ```js function createTopo(){ const bytes=heightmaps[0]||[]; const
2986fbdd [roha]    heightmap=AllocBumpmap(bytes); heightmap.setBytes(bytes); const n=heightmaps.length; for(let
2986fbdd [roha]    i=1;i<n;i++){ if(heightmaps[i].length==bytes.length){ heightmap.orBytes(heightmaps[i]); } ...
2986fbdd [roha]    } } ``` Two important caveats about that path: - `orBytes` is **overwrite where nonzero**, not
2986fbdd [roha]    max or blend. It only writes where the incoming source has `h != 0`. So order in `sourceList`
2986fbdd [roha]    decides who wins — and that order is just the order of
2986fbdd [roha]    `tiles.ae
